in the real world we are trapped in our  own bodies contained within our own  appearance now sure we can tweak our  physical shape through diet and exercise  or we can cut and style our hair but  ultimately we have very little control  over how we actually look to other  people not so in the world of video  games though the ability to customize  your character or avatar has long been a  staple of role-playing adventure games  in fact one of the finest creation  engines I've ever used is found in  Dragon Age Inquisition the latest entry  into the fantasy epic series from  Bioware certainly playing with these  creators and crafting the ideal hero is  a lot of fun but why is enjoyable and  perhaps more interestingly why do we  create the characters that we do  [Music]  to find the answers to these questions I  started by asking a few of my lovely  colleagues here at the CBS Interactive  London office to have a blast at making  a character in the Dragon Age  Inquisition editor naturally I filmed  the process so kick back have a watch  and maybe ask yourself if you can see  any similarities between what you do in  character creation and what these guys  said when you've got all of the options  available to you I think I naturally go  towards trying to build me I kind of  always am a character that has essence  of me but then is better than I am  better cheekbones than I am  generally they don't know that they're  not a short or weedy in video game land  so it's kind of like me if I was put  into an automaton machine or something  like that  it's my wife so creating something that  looks a bit like her although sometimes  that goes badly wrong I think sometimes  you sort of fall into the uncanny valley  and it just looks dreadful gonna go full  two-handed warriors I like the idea of  just attacking without defending is the  option to give myself a bum chin because  of the bum bum insider  up to you on your chin to look like I'd  like it to look quite like a bum gels no  I tend to name my characters after war  leaders so yeah so Napoleon Churchill  things like that done basil basil the  destroyer he is ready drew gone I'm  going with drew God  now as some of you might have guessed  one of these people isn't actually a  colleague of mine in fact Mark Wilson is  a reader in psychology at the University  of Middlesex and as long-running reality  check fans will surely remember a  regular expert on the show so I sat down  for a proper chat with Mark to get the  detailed psychology angle on all of this  and also to try and figure out exactly  what's going on inside my weird  colleagues brains a really attractive  idea when we're creating an avatar to  create an ideal version of our own self  so that's something core someone rather  than who is more attractive stronger  more athletic looking one of the  problems with that type of idea is that  if you create someone which is too ideal  too heroic and to godlike then you could  actually lose the sense of immersion  immersion requires it to be some sort of  physical or psychological link between  you and the avatar that you're playing  if that distance is too great then this  is no longer me it's somebody else many  people want to maintain the illusion  that when you play the game this is not  you driving a character this is you and  that's an important distinction because  if you consider your behavior in game to  be your behavior not just some Souls  digital creation then the the plot the  actions and the moral decisions the  emotional effects can be far more real  now when I build characters I don't  usually try to recreate myself in the  game however I have found that I do tend  to include a very few specific  attributes of me in my characters for  example I used to give my avatars curly  hair back when I had that and now I  notice myself frequently creating heroes  with shaved heads so I asked mark if  including specific characteristics of  ourselves in characters perhaps ones we  don't really love could really all be  about trying to validate our own  real-world appearance  one of the things we pretty much know  when we create a game character is that  this this avatar is going to bludgeon  kill or fireball its way through  hundreds or even thousands of opponent  so it's it's a god-like creation now if  we can see a short skinny bald godlike  creation doing that and that's what we  may perceive ourselves as becoming this  is quite a psychologically healthy thing  to do the fact is is that as as physical  entities as physical cells we are  trapped within our bodies we have some  control over them but but really it's  very limited what online worlds allow us  to do is to take complete control over  our appearance and allow our psychology  to drive our behavior what we do tend to  find is the people who are more  comfortable in themselves don't have  such strong preferences for creating  heroic or beautiful avatars similarly  people who score high on the personality  dimension of agreeableness that is  they're they're very easygoing they get  on well with people tend to create  avatars that are less ideal than people  who score low on this personality  tension now as Mark alluded to not  everybody creates a character resembling  themselves in any way and if this  describes you then fear not on next  week's episode I'm gonna do a part two  of the psychology of character creation  focusing on creating different  characters including why as much as 30%  of men choose to play as women and also  have the character you create can  actually have an impact on your own life  and real-world behavior so make sure you  come back next week for that and in the  meantime why not leave me a comment  about this episode in the box down below  or of course you can send me a tweet at  cam for us Rob okay folks thanks for  watching see you next week  you  [Music]
 

